#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511644} :activity/id #uuid "555fcac9-8713-58b2-9317-3fe48ec69606" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050730 :topic/id #uuid "621645f8-2215-52be-860a-5546f675b962" :topic/math-academy-id 6709} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151c2adc-f498-5355-bc1b-efa89a51b45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510827} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4b8479-723c-55f8-8a13-809141b3b771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "sine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511011}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511011} :answer-field/id #uuid "3130d5c0-7c5e-597f-aea9-6bb1181000b3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b810400-5899-5b7e-9373-4174747a47a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511036} {:answer/id #uuid "b69ee136-7e7e-535f-917e-01a32e6e673d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511159}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511159} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b911936-49df-53cf-9f01-74e0cebc1ad3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c279eae-dd78-5b67-912d-9a38a48eda28" :question/math-academy-id "q-340800" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the {{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To check whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =7-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ we have that $f$ is even.\n\nFor the Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is even and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd, so $f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)$ is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${b}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{even}$ and the $\\text{sine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."} :step/id #uuid "59e44bcc-0c2e-57a7-8e71-45cfd8dd1e87" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511638 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31897f5a-6e6b-5449-8ab3-590db3d42de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{a}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510886} {:answer/id #uuid "919e0113-edd3-5841-879e-86aa411c1a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{b}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511081} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7aa80e-855d-54f5-a677-6ef4cbb64763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{5}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511146} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0e1606-796a-518e-aacf-18478af80e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511236} {:answer/id #uuid "fffdf104-47aa-5883-820c-6129aeff7de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511308} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cd3b6c4-428e-5510-88f8-cbc092e81e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f0bd5207-abfa-5ab3-9c43-fac18e98df50" :question/math-academy-id "q-340784" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $5$th cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $5$th cosine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{5}.$"} :step/id #uuid "8c2976be-ba7a-5dd8-a390-f8f1e5aa8929" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e664224-e5b9-5dea-89e9-5258fe549e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510809} {:answer/id #uuid "3003975d-8590-52c8-854f-7b40173950e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510884} {:answer/id #uuid "79b9b20a-a095-5016-8fe7-3f0217a39664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511032} {:answer/id #uuid "cee4def6-0cfa-56ad-99c4-7b9846ee4316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511211} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e53dcb-9b26-58de-96f2-fc3f376f55dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511211} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9d73a9-94dc-5fe0-87cb-3e57fcd56b5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "270a37f5-30dc-586c-8bce-5a858bce0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340776" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that if $C$ is a constant, the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(x)+C$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}+2C$  for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- ${b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nIn our case, $C=4.$ Therefore, the constant Fourier coefficient of  $f(x)+4$ is ${a}_{0}+8.$"} :step/id #uuid "9c211d01-9532-5ebc-88b5-b6935d393a65" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16acd564-8bb4-5f1c-82c3-561b03904c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312131} {:answer/id #uuid "66e3422f-b052-5204-9993-f9b920cdcc5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{a}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312787} {:answer/id #uuid "ac180436-b1eb-556d-a68d-e61a5de3235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{b}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313323} {:answer/id #uuid "d2744563-6a41-5893-93d0-b2e699ab8ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313621} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d54e34-b41d-5b58-b634-d28765ea5ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{b}_{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313621} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57dbd7a-67e4-5987-80bd-70ad3b4f1683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71f49ef2-3b80-5e3d-890f-74bbd2f89ef7" :question/math-academy-id "q-340782" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of a function from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R$ with the Fourier series shown above. What is the $4$th cosine Fourier coefficient of $f(-x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nRecall that the corresponding Fourier coefficients of $f(-x)$ are\n\n- ${a}_{0}$ for the constant coefficient,\n\n- ${a}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the cosine coefficients, and\n\n- $-{b}_{n},$ $n\\ge 1,$ for the sine coefficients.\n\nTherefore, the $4$th cosine coefficient of $f(-x)$ is ${a}_{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "b2b11a51-d093-57da-a2af-6ee6f4f4690b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511638}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a0583c-ea43-573f-b06b-b4a00d3b2c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511020} {:answer/id #uuid "f5668d0f-beb2-5460-abcc-92d140689ec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511290}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511290} :answer-field/id #uuid "19bcbcdc-3036-501a-8695-55f2eed0f886" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511325} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8459b090-9382-5cc1-a414-5ca52e5a7ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "cosine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511053} {:answer/id #uuid "d9aad8a1-e2f8-56c6-bcac-8497da0fc04a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "sine terms" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511053} :answer-field/id #uuid "a035b7e1-0b84-5941-bd5f-075b05041e0e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd373585-64b2-5a1f-ad55-f3dee081e651" :question/math-academy-id "q-340808" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ Select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function $f(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$\nConsequently, the \n{{field-2}}\nin the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$ must be zero." :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f$ is even or odd, we compare $f(-x)$ and $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ we have that $f$ is odd.\n\nFor Fourier series on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ],$ the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nand the constant coefficient is\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHere, $f(x)$ is odd and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is even, so $f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)$ is odd. Also, $f(x)$ itself is odd.\n\nNow, the integral of an odd function over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is $0.$ Thus, ${a}_{n}=0$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{0}=0.$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is $\\text{odd}$ and the $\\text{cosine terms}$ in its Fourier series must be zero."} :step/id #uuid "e2232810-d75c-5b33-8c6d-124abfa15358"}] :activity/title "Properties of Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510759}]}
